      v
Public website (GitHub source, deployed on Render)
      |
      +---- Public catalogue reads ----> Supabase (existing project)
      |                                  |
      |                                  +-- RLS allows only approved public catalogue fields
      |
      +---- Gift Builder / quote enquiry ----> Server-side API
      |                                        |
      |                                        +-- Validate and limit requests
      |                                        +-- Keep AI keys on server
      |                                        +-- Send enquiry to an approved destination
      |
      +---- AI concept request ----------> AI provider through server API
                                               |
                                               +-- Return concept text/image
Read-only database boundary
The requested website should read the same existing database for catalogue information. Treat “read-only” as the public website's database permission boundary, not merely a promise in frontend code.
- Use a publishable/anonymous key only for catalogue reads that are explicitly allowed by RLS.
- Never put SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY, a service-role key, AI provider keys, or other privileged credentials in client-side JavaScript or a public GitHub repository.
- Do not grant public writes to customers, quotes, orders, expenses, or other internal tables just to implement enquiries.
- If the customer submits a Gift Builder enquiry, use a server-side endpoint with validation and abuse protection. It may send an email or store the enquiry only through a separately approved, narrowly scoped mechanism.
- If enquiry persistence is required, agree on the destination first: a protected server-side workflow, a dedicated public-enquiry table with strict policies, or the existing app's authenticated API if a suitable endpoint exists. Do not assume that endpoint already exists.
- Public order tracking, if added later, must use a secure token or authenticated customer access and must never expose another customer's order.
Suggested deployment separation
- GitHub: version-controlled source repository; use a private repository if the source should not be public.
- Render: deploy the website/service from GitHub; configure environment variables in Render.
- Supabase: existing data and approved public catalogue reads.
- AI provider: accessed only from a server-side endpoint.
- Image storage: use existing public product assets only when they are intended for public display; do not expose private storage buckets.
The README notes that Render Free services can sleep after 15 minutes of inactivity and the first request may take around a minute. Confirm current Render plan behavior and suitability before production. A storefront intended for customers may need a paid always-on service for consistent response times. Static hosting is possible only if the implementation is genuinely static; AI secret handling and protected server endpoints require a server-side component.
8. Security, privacy, and reliability
- Keep the existing management app's private tables and APIs inaccessible to public visitors.
- Verify Supabase RLS using unauthenticated test requests.
- Validate all user input server-side; apply rate limits and abuse prevention to AI and enquiry endpoints.
- Set spending limits and request quotas for AI features where available.
- Avoid sending customer contact details or sensitive information to an AI provider unless necessary and disclosed.
- Provide privacy notice and consent language appropriate to the final implementation.
- Do not expose private purchase proofs, customer records, staff information, affiliate ledgers, or internal financial data.
- Do not commit .env files or API keys.
- Log operational errors without logging secrets or unnecessary personal data.
- Add automated tests for public catalogue access, private data denial, input validation, and API failure handling.
9. Scope boundaries
Included in initial release
- Brand-led responsive homepage.
- Public product and collection catalogue connected to Supabase.
- Product detail pages.
- Custom Gift Builder with guided choices.
- AI-generated text suggestions and a proposed AI concept-image flow, subject to provider selection and budget.
- Contact/quotation enquiry submission through an approved server-side workflow.
- Corporate gifting landing page.
- Discovery Friends integration.
- Basic analytics, SEO metadata, accessibility, error handling, and deployment setup.
Not assumed for initial release
- Online payment and checkout.
- Live stock reservation.
- Guaranteed delivery dates or delivery fee calculation.
- Customer accounts or order history.
- Automatic creation of orders/quotes inside the management app.
- Real-time synchronization beyond catalogue reads.
- Full interactive 3D product configurator.
- AI image generation without provider selection, cost limits, and API implementation.
- CRM/marketing automation beyond agreed enquiry routing.
These can be added after the initial site and workflows are validated.
10. Delivery phases
Phase 0 — Discovery and technical validation
- Inspect the current Supabase catalogue schema, RLS policies, public image URLs, product/collection fields, and existing server API.
- Confirm public pricing rules, supported customizations, contact details, languages, and quotation process.
- Choose the AI text/image provider and define cost limits.
- Confirm Render deployment plan and domain.
Deliverable: approved data map and technical decisions.
Phase 1 — Brand storefront
- Build navigation, homepage, catalogue, collections, product pages, corporate gifts, and contact page.
- Connect public catalogue reads to Supabase.
- Apply the logo, palette, and mascot system.
- Test responsive behavior, accessibility, performance, and security.
Deliverable: public storefront deployed to a preview environment.
Phase 2 — Gift Builder
- Implement guided gift selection, summary, selected product list, budget guidance, and enquiry handoff.
- Add AI text suggestions and controlled catalogue-grounded recommendations.
- Add concept-image generation if the provider and budget are approved.
- Add disclaimers, input validation, rate limits, and error states.
Deliverable: tested Gift Builder with enquiry workflow.
Phase 3 — Launch readiness
- Connect production domain and HTTPS.
- Verify RLS and server-side secret handling.
- Test on mobile and desktop.
- Test empty catalogue, unavailable AI provider, slow responses, invalid input, and deployment restart.
- Add privacy/terms pages, analytics, backups/monitoring plan, and operational ownership.
Deliverable: production launch checklist and live website.
Phase 4 — Optional 3D and commerce expansion
- Create a small library of actual 3D box/product assets.
- Build interactive placement, rotation, and supported customization.
- Consider checkout/payment, customer accounts, order tracking, and deeper management-app integration only after process decisions are finalized.
11. Acceptance criteria
The initial release is accepted when:
- Visitors can browse only approved public catalogue items.
- Product and collection data come from the existing Supabase source of truth.
- No private management data or privileged keys are accessible from the browser.
- The Gift Builder guides a user from occasion/preferences to a reviewable gift concept.
- AI output is clearly marked as suggestions and is grounded in the available catalogue.
- The user can submit an enquiry through the approved server-side workflow.
- The website works at mobile, tablet, and desktop widths.
- The logo, colors, and mascots are applied consistently.
- Loading, empty, invalid-input, and provider-failure states are handled.
- Deployment survives a restart without depending on local persistent files.
- Basic security, accessibility, and performance checks pass.
12. Decisions required before implementation
1. Language: English, Portuguese, or both at launch?
2. Pricing: show public prices, “request a quote,” or both depending on product?
3. Enquiry destination: email only initially, or persist enquiries somewhere that staff can process?
4. Gift preview: start with AI-generated 2D concept images, then add true interactive 3D later?
5. AI budget: what monthly budget or maximum cost per generated concept is acceptable?
6. Public catalogue: which products and collections are approved for display, and are prices/images complete?
7. Hosting: is Render Free acceptable for a prototype, or is an always-on production service required?
13. Final recommendation
Build the storefront and Gift Builder as one cohesive website, with Supabase as the read-only source for approved public catalogue data. Launch first with guided gift creation and AI-assisted concepts; treat true interactive 3D as a separate phase requiring real 3D assets. Keep all privileged operations and AI credentials on the server, and make the enquiry workflow explicit rather than silently writing to the internal management database.
This approach creates a distinctive, mascot-led customer experience while protecting the existing business management app and leaving room for future e-commerce capabilities.
